yourself you don't have to go through  the mortgage application yourself you're  not in hundreds of thousands of dollars  in debt yourself and an antidote to a  so-called epidemic of loneliness  exacerbated by the pandemic we have  great neighbors where we are right now  but it would be  even better to take that kind of the  next level and have really close  neighbors that you can maybe rely on and  they can rely on you the best of both  worlds all under one roof and gaviola  global news toronto  ahead the battle to save lives in  canada's deadliest epidemic  it's an epidemic many canadians would  rather ignore for years the opioid  crisis has shattered families and  communities across the country taking  lives in every age and socio-economic  demographic since 2016 more than 29 000  people have died as a result of drug  toxicity as rachel gilmore reports  advocates on the front lines of this  epidemic are once again pushing for more  awareness hoping it will save lives  every day 21 canadians die from opioid  overdoses the frantic fight to save  lives is being waged across the country  it's impossible  any day of the week to not hear about  somebody who has died but there are  concerns years into this opioid crisis  the battle is being lost in silence  everything but families of overdose  victims and canadian senator vern white  want to raise public awareness about the  severity of the crisis they're asking  prime minister justin trudeau to lower  the flag on parliament hill to  acknowledge those who die each day so  far that hasn't happened including this  week during international overdose  awareness day i don't hold a lot of hope  i think actually that  many are afraid to talk about it they  they don't want to get into the  discussion about who is impacted  in the first full year of the pandemic  alone there was a 96 percent increase in  opioid deaths nationally with more than  7 500 deaths in 2021 alone it's  impossible wendy muckle is the ceo of  inner city health which operates a safe  consumption site in ottawa she has  experienced the cruelty of the opioid  crisis firsthand it's like we're in a  war inside this whole other world and  nobody else really knows that we're at  war  she says there are concrete steps all  levels of government can take to better  address this growing problem including  decriminalizing possession of small  amounts of drugs  chad boutilier works at a safe  consumption site while he supports calls  to draw more public attention to opioid  deaths he says action is also needed  lowering a flag is is not going to stop  people dying  i think a lot of things have to happen  in a statement the government did not  acknowledge the calls to lower the flag  instead noting several buildings across  canada were flooded with purple light  wednesday to increase overdose awareness  and ottawa isn't committing to any new  action  advocates are urging the government to  move now because every day that they  wait for changes more canadians are  dying rachel gilmore global news ottawa  the second launch attempt  of the space launch system  and orion spacecraft  has been scrubbed  a fuel leak was blamed for once again  keeping nasa's new artemis one moon  rocket grounded today the test flight is  part of the space agency's renewed lunar  exploration goals in which canada is  playing a key role  this is our team swan the first of a  series of missions the artemis program  the second flight with crew is going to  have a canadian space agency astronaut  on board so  huge for our nation  this first uncrewed mission has until  tuesday to take off or face being  delayed until october  one incredible journey has come to an  end for one of the world's biggest  sports stars tennis icon serena williams  has played what is likely her last  professional match and she did not go  quietly the 23-time grand slam champion  was defeated in the third round of the  u.s open after a grueling three-hour  game kamel carameli looks back on serena  williams game-changing career  the end of a dream run at the u.s open  leading to the end of an era  just  everyone that's here that's been on my  side so many years decades oh my gosh  literally decades after 23 grand slam  singles titles the most by any player in  the open era serena williams hung up her  racket for good friday and the tributes  came pouring in from the king of the  basketball court it's unprecedented and  um it's been an honor  to watch your journey to women's tennis  legend billie jean king her incredible  career made its mark on tennis history  to a new generation hoping to follow in  her footsteps it's because of you i  believe in this dream former u.s first  lady michelle obama also tweeting how  lucky were we to be able to watch a  young girl from compton grow up to  become one of the greatest athletes of  all time serena's impact on canadian  soil undeniable winning the canadian  title three times the last one happening  in 2013.  still the last american woman to win in  canada she's played our tournament for  over 20 years  and i see all the young kids that she  influences here we go  for young canadians watching her win  also meant watching the game change more  diversity and inclusion on the tennis  court i just looked up to her all the  time she  like being a woman of color i don't  think she's leaving a whole i think  she's living like a legacy canadian  teens who weren't even born when  williams won her first grand slam title  now hoping to learn from that legacy  even when she stops now i feel like  she's going to keep inspiring  generations to come  you know for her to come from absolutely  nothing and to achieve the success that  she has is uh very inspiring now the 40  year old plans to grow her family and  her other business ventures building a  different legacy off the court kamal  karamali global news that's global  national for this saturday night i'm  colleen christie tonight your canada is  this beautiful time lapse of the sunset  at vancouver sunset beach of course we  love seeing your canada please keep  emailing your photos to viewers at  globalnational.com  thanks for watching i hope to see you  back here again tomorrow good night  [Music]
 

